+to create virtual HID devices. Keyboard, mouse and pen input use CoreGraphics
+and require the normal
+macOS synthetic-input permission (Accessibility). The pen backend emits tablet
+proximity and point events, including tip pressure, two barrel buttons, eraser
+identity and tilt clamped to the AppKit consumer range of +/-60 degrees. It is
+event injection, not a registered HID tablet; applications that enumerate
+physical tablet drivers may not recognize it. Touchscreen and trackpad creation
+remain unavailable. Pen injection does not require the gamepad broker/license.
+
+Pen viewports are expressed in CoreGraphics **screen points**, not Retina
+backing pixels; a zero-sized viewport follows the current main display. Pass
+capture display bounds when targeting another monitor. Close/cancel/leave
+release held buttons and retire proximity. Invalid non-finite updates are
+rejected. A client must send pen events, not finger touch or mouse emulation.
+
+Native event conversion and Qt's tablet handlers are covered by local tests.
+End-to-end OS delivery and physical stylus compatibility still require a host
+with input permission and a pen-capable client. See
+[macOS setup](macos-gamepad.md) for gamepad installation and diagnostics.
 
